## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses practical enterprise RAG architecture with multi-tenant isolation, policy governance, and regulatory compliance across global regions. It tests understanding of data locality requirements, policy lifecycle management, and comprehensive testing strategies—critical components for production LLM deployments serving Fortune 500 clients.

## Key Concepts
- Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) with tenant isolation
- Region-aware routing and data locality enforcement
- Policy versioning with TTLs and automated eviction
- Vector store segmentation and encryption
- Compliance testing (GDPR/CCPA) and drift detection

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: enforce tenant-scoped lookups and region routing
function routeQuery(req) {
  const { tenantId, region } = req;
  const policyVersion = getCurrentPolicyVersion(tenantId);
  const vectorStore = getTenantVectorStore(`${tenantId}_${region}`);

  if (!isCompliantWithPolicy(req, policyVersion)) {
    throw new PolicyViolationError();
  }

  return vectorStore.search(req.query, {
    tenantId,
    policyVersion,
    dataMinimization: true
  });
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to balance cost, latency, and privacy through dynamic routing and policy binding; requires understanding canary deployments, rollbacks, and observability patterns.

## Key Concepts
- Cost- and latency-aware routing algorithms
- Per-request policy bindings
- Real-time risk-budget scoring
- Data locality and quota enforcement
- Canary deployment and rollback mechanisms

## Code Example

```javascript
function selectModel(riskBudget, latencyMs, quotas) {
  // naive decision rules for demo
  if (latencyMs > quotas.maxLatency) return 'privacy-gate';
  if (riskBudget > quotas.highRisk) return 'distilled';
  return 'base';
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Real-world needs balance cost, latency, and reliability across tenants. This asks for a concrete policy language, a routing strategy, and test plans under varying traffic. It also probes drift handling and failover.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant QoS policies
- Multi-model routing with cost/latency prediction
- Data locality and failover strategies
- Observability: audit logs, drift tests

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo routing sketch
function route(req, tenant) {
  const p = policy[tenant];
  const candidates = models.filter(m => p.preferredModels.includes(m.name));
  // pick cheapest that meets latency
  return candidates
    .map(m => ({m, est: predict(m, req)}))
    .filter(x => x.est.lat <= p.max_latency)
    .sort((a,b) => a.est.cost - b.est.cost)[0]?.m;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement drift detection for provider costs?
- How would you test circuit-breaker behavior under bursty traffic?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design realtime, tenant-aware safety controls in LLM-ops with auditable trails and rollback.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant baselines (prompts/outputs)
- Real-time anomaly detection with low latency
- Mitigations: quarantine, guarded routing, human review
- Immutable audit logs and rollback capability
- Explainability hooks for decisions

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo detector sketch
function detect(inputEmb, tenantId){
  const baseline = getBaseline(tenantId);
  const score = cosine(inputEmb, baseline);
  return score > THRESHOLD;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you measure latency impact for each mitigation path?
- How would you ensure audit tamper-evidence across tenants?
